---
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Sessions 24&ndash;25: Iterate &amp; Re-calibrate</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Racquet Ball Launcher Project &mdash; Milestone 4 of 6</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <h2>Small Improvements Only</h2>
          <p>
            This is a stated project rule, not a fallback for teams that
            ran out of time. The point isn't a bigger redesign &mdash; it's
            tightening your model where it was weakest, the same
            "Iterate &ne; starting over" idea from Session 1.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your Session 21&ndash;23 model is already solid, "improvement"
            can mean tightening precision at the edges of your usable range
            instead of changing anything mechanical.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Bungee safety still applies.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Identify Your Weakest Point</h3>
          <p>
            Look at your Session 21&ndash;23 calibration data. Where was the
            model weakest or least precise &mdash; widest scatter, lowest
            confidence at one edge of your usable range, a setting your
            conditional formatting flagged?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Make One Targeted Change</h3>
          <p>
            Change one specific thing aimed at that weakness. Not a
            redesign &mdash; a targeted adjustment, the same distinction
            Session 1 made between Iterate and starting over.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Re-Test and Re-Plot</h3>
          <p>
            Re-run trials after your change, and update your calibration
            curve: new plot, new trendline, new R&sup2;, updated usable
            range.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Log the Change</h3>
          <p>In your notebook, record:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>What changed</li>
            <li>Why (tied to the specific weakness you targeted)</li>
            <li>How the calibration curve shifted as a result</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If your re-test shows no improvement, or a slightly worse fit,
            that's still valid data &mdash; document it honestly rather than
            quietly discarding it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Your notebook entry must show all of the following:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>A specific weakness named &mdash; not a generic "we improved it"</li>
            <li>A change that's small and targeted, not a mechanical redesign</li>
            <li>Updated calibration curve with new R&sup2; and usable range, even if the result didn't improve</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Did your targeted change actually improve your model, make it
              worse, or leave it about the same? What does that outcome
              tell you?
            </li>
            <li>
              Why do you think this project's rule is "small changes only"
              instead of allowing a full redesign at this stage?
            </li>
            <li>
              Your usable range from today is what you'll rely on for real
              on Field Test Day. Are you confident in it, or is there a
              distance range you'd still avoid being tested on if you could
              choose?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your updated notebook entry, updated calibration curve, and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Milestone Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
